WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.220
Today we are going to have a&nbsp;
peek into DeepMind’s journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.120
into creating an AI that behaves like a human.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:18.960
How? Well, by dropping it into a virtual&nbsp;
playhouse, asking humans to give it tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.180
judging it rather harshly, and hoping&nbsp;
that it learns something from it.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:30.540
But, not so fast! First, we have to start&nbsp;
with an AI agent, a little assistant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:37.500
that already knows a few things. Just dropping&nbsp;
a randomly acting agent into this game would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.800
do no good. So, how do we do that? Well,&nbsp;
scientists at DeepMind say that first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:51.480
we should ask it to look at humans interacting&nbsp;
with previous AI agents. And have it copy them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:57.240
Let’s call this behavioral cloning.&nbsp;
So, how good is behavioral cloning?

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:03.900
Well, the answer is not good at all. Let’s have&nbsp;
a look together. This agent has looked at humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:11.100
building towers from books, and now, it’s your&nbsp;
turn, little AI. And it does… not too well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:17.640
I am afraid. Oh my. That is not good.&nbsp;
Look. It has learned a bit from humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:24.300
it is trying so hard, but unfortunately&nbsp;
it has no idea about how physics works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.200
and does not have the finesse&nbsp;
required to build this tower.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:36.180
So, is that it? We can’t even write an AI that&nbsp;
clones what humans can already perform in virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:43.200
worlds? Well, do not despair, because here comes&nbsp;
the second best part of the paper. In this work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:50.040
this agent is used just as a starting point,&nbsp;
and now, we can ask it to do a bunch of tasks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.260
and have humans give it a score. For&nbsp;
instance, here you see it trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.260 --> 00:02:02.040
arrange these fruits in a row. And, whenever&nbsp;
it progresses with this task, it gets a nice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:08.220
spike in the score. And whenever it does not do&nbsp;
well, we can tell it that this is not the way.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:15.420
Alright, but what does this process do for us?&nbsp;
Well, it creates an agent that can learn not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:22.560
the behavior of humans, but the reward model&nbsp;
itself. What does that mean? Well, it tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:30.120
learn what the human teacher would think about&nbsp;
what it is doing right now. And, is that useful?

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:36.780
Well, have a look at it yourself. Here is the&nbsp;
agent after it started to learn the reward model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:44.040
and, let’s build that tower again. First&nbsp;
try is not too bad, but the second one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:53.940
now look at that. That is a proper tower of&nbsp;
books right there. Very proud of you, little AI!

00:02:53.940 --> 00:03:01.020
So, how well did it do? Here is the score&nbsp;
for a human player. Here it is for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:06.840
not too great first try with the behavioral&nbsp;
cloning AI. And now, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:16.200
and look at that! This is the new agent. It does&nbsp;
almost as well as a real human does. Wow. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:22.140
an amazing chart that shows that these agents&nbsp;
can learn tasks that require walking around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:28.680
understanding a bit of physics, and&nbsp;
a bit of finesse as well. I love it.&nbsp;

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:35.580
And, it can learn not just building towers, but&nbsp;
it also learned to build triangles of objects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:42.360
and of course, everyone’s favorite. What&nbsp;
good would an AI be if we could not teach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:49.800
it to clean our room? So, can it do that?&nbsp;
Oh yes, and with flying colors. Loving it.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:56.760
But wait, I said that this was the second&nbsp;
best part of the paper. What is the best part?

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:04.620
It is this. Remember, first, we start out from&nbsp;
the behavioral cloning agent, and start learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:11.280
the reward model. This does nearly as well as&nbsp;
a human does, so that is absolutely amazing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:19.200
we can teach an AI to behave like a human does in&nbsp;
these tasks. This is incredible. But that’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:26.880
it. Get this, if we repeat this process and&nbsp;
let it learn for a bit longer, we get this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:34.620
Whoa! Are you seeing what I am seeing? Yes, that&nbsp;
is right, if we let it train for a bit longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:41.940
this happens. It can even surpass humans at&nbsp;
building that tower. An AI that learns from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:50.340
humans, and over time, even surpasses humans.&nbsp;
And, wait, it gets better, it surpasses humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:59.220
not just on this one particular task, but on&nbsp;
a set of over a 150 standardized tasks. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:05.760
they are. Generally, it greatly outperforms in&nbsp;
instruction following-type tasks, while it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:13.200
a bit worse than humans at answering questions,&nbsp;
but it still does really well. Wow. Perhaps this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:20.460
is one more step towards general intelligence.&nbsp;
And also, welcome to Two Minute Papers, Land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:27.900
of the Fellow Scholars who look at bar charts and&nbsp;
make happy noises. Yeah! What a time to be alive!

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:27.720
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

